---
import LabCard from '../../components/LabCard.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLabs } from '../../lib/labs';

const labs = await getLabs();
---

<Base
	title="Labs"
	description="Labs that compare a bare agent with a sandboxed one. Lab 01, Secret Exfiltration, is coming soon."
	current="labs"
>
	<section class="section lab">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="kicker">Index</p>
			<h1>Labs</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Educate by doing. Each lab attacks a bare agent, repeats the attack inside a sandbox, and scores the
				difference. All lab attacks run only in isolated, local lab environments.
			</p>
			<ul class="lab-grid">
				{
					labs.map((lab) => (
						<li>
							<LabCard lab={lab} level={2} />
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>

			<div class="panel author">
				<h2>Add a lab</h2>
				<p>
					A lab is one markdown file. The build checks the headings, so a new page picks up the same shape as the
					<a href="/labs/template/">page template</a>.
				</p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						Copy <code>site/src/content/labs/template.md</code> to <code>site/src/content/labs/NN-short-name.md</code>.
						<code>NN</code> is the next two-digit number. The file name is the URL, so
						<code>04-egress-notes.md</code> is served at <code>/labs/04-egress-notes/</code>.
					</li>
					<li>
						Set <code>title</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>status</code>
						(<code>coming-soon</code> or <code>available</code>), and <code>kind: lab</code>.
					</li>
					<li>
						Keep these seven level-2 headings, in order: Objectives, Prerequisites, Setup, Attack, Defend, Measure,
						Cleanup. Replace the template guidance with the lab.
					</li>
					<li>
						From <code>site/</code>, run <code>npm run build</code>. The build fails if a heading is missing, or if
						site copy contains an em dash or an en dash.
					</li>
				</ol>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
